    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was the province of Britannia, covering most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from the Claudian invasion of 43 until about 410.</li><li>It held one of the largest garrisons in the empire, guarding a northern frontier marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>.</li><li>Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a>, a road network, villas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> were its lasting legacies.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> was proclaimed emperor at York in 306, after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius</a> died there while campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

