    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> came, as a source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and as the island across the Ocean from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> crossed twice, in 55 and 54 BCE, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassivellaunus">Cassivellaunus</a> and taking hostages and promises of tribute, but he left no garrison. For the next century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> dealt with British rulers through diplomacy, and southern kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> of the Catuvellauni struck coins and imported wine and fine pottery from the empire. In 43 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, seeking military prestige, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius">Aulus Plautius</a> with four legions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">II Augusta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ix-hispana">IX Hispana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-xiv-gemina">XIV Gemina</a> and XX, and auxiliaries, perhaps 40,000 men in all. After battles on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">Thames</a>, Claudius himself arrived to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Camulodunum</a> (Colchester), the Catuvellaunian centre, and his triumphal arch in Rome recorded the surrender of eleven British kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a> became the first capital and, from 49, a colony of veterans. Some peoples, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regni">Regni</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togidubnus">Togidubnus</a> and the Iceni under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a>, were left as client kingdoms, while Vespasian, commanding the II Augusta, conquered the south-west. Within four years a frontier ran roughly along the line later followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fosse-way">Fosse Way</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/colchester"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colchester.jpg" alt="Colchester" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colchester_Castle_(geograph_4444025).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dr Neil Clifton, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> remained a largely rural society, with a population often estimated, very approximately, at two to three million. Most people lived in farmsteads and villages much like those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, but a network of towns grew up: coloniae of veterans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, and civitas capitals for the native peoples, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Corinium</a> (Cirencester) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum">Viroconium</a> (Wroxeter). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a>, founded around 50 at the lowest bridging point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">Thames</a>, became the largest city, with a forum and basilica that were among the biggest north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. In the countryside the elite built villas, from modest farmhouses to the great palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fishbourne-roman-palace">Fishbourne</a> of about 75, and in the 4th century houses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chedworth-roman-villa">Chedworth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bignor-roman-villa">Bignor</a> reached their greatest size and finest mosaics. The province exported grain, wool, hunting dogs, lead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendip-hills">Mendips</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>; the British woollen cloak, the <em>birrus Britannicus</em>, appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s price edict of 301. Religion mixed Roman and Celtic cults, as at the spring of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulis-minerva">Sulis Minerva</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, where some 130 curse tablets survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread from the 3rd century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinton-st-mary-mosaic">Hinton St Mary</a> mosaic shows an early image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Christ</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius.jpg" alt="Battle of Mons Graupius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius">Battle of Mons Graupius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agricola.Campaigns.80.84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">myself, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> held one of the largest garrisons in the empire, perhaps a tenth of the whole Roman army in the 2nd century. Resistance was fierce at first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> led the Silures and Ordovices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> until his defeat in 51, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a>, queen of the Brigantes, handed him over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. In 60 or 61, while the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> was attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> of the Iceni rose in revolt and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Camulodunum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> before she was defeated somewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>. Under Vespasian the advance resumed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a>, governor from about 77 to 84, campaigned deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and defeated the Caledonians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius">Mons Graupius</a> in 83 or 84. From the late 1st century three legions remained, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerleon">Caerleon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chester">Chester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, with auxiliary forts across Wales and the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>, begun around 122 and running some 73 miles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">Tyne</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway</a>, became the main frontier; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-clyde">Clyde</a>, built from about 142, was held for only some twenty years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> campaigned in Scotland from 208 and died at York in 211. In the late empire a chain of coastal forts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Shore, guarded the south-east against seaborne raiders.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/boudica"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boudica.jpg" alt="Boudica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Boudica_by_John_Opie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Opie, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was an imperial province governed by a <em>legatus Augusti pro praetore</em>, a senator of consular rank chosen by the emperor, who commanded the army and administered justice. Finance was the business of a separate procurator; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>'s revolt the procurator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-classicianus">Julius Classicianus</a> urged a milder policy, and his tombstone was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Local government was left to the civitates, self-governing communities based on the old tribal peoples, whose councils of landowners ran towns, collected taxes and maintained roads such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermine-street">Ermine Street</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fosse-way">Fosse Way</a>. Client kingdoms were absorbed once their rulers died. To limit the power of a single governor with three legions, the province was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britannia-superior">Britannia Superior</a>, ruled from London, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britannia-inferior">Britannia Inferior</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, a reform traditionally credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> but perhaps completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms about 300 there were four provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britannia-prima">Britannia Prima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britannia-secunda">Britannia Secunda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxima-caesariensis">Maxima Caesariensis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavia-caesariensis">Flavia Caesariensis</a>, later joined by Valentia, grouped in a diocese under a <em>vicarius</em> based in London. Military command passed to new officers, the Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a> and the Count of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Shore. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindolanda">Vindolanda</a> tablets, letters on wood from a fort near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>, reveal the routine of this administration around 100.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall.jpg" alt="Hadrian's Wall" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadrians_Wall_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Created by NormanEinstein, September 20, 2005; derivative work by Talifero, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was the empire's north-western frontier for more than three and a half centuries, and it played a large part in imperial politics. Its armies made emperors: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clodius-albinus">Clodius Albinus</a> in 196, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a>, who ruled Britain and part of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> as an independent emperor from 286 to 293, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, proclaimed at York in 306 on the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius</a>. Britain shared in the life of the empire; three British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-arles">Council of Arles</a> in 314, and the theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelagius</a> came from the island. The physical legacy is still visible. Many English towns stand on Roman sites, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chester">Chester</a>, whose names often preserve their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> forms. Roman roads remained routes for centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. Latin left fewer traces in the language than in Gaul, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement replaced much of the population's speech in the east, but Brittonic took Latin loanwords that survive in Welsh, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> survived in the west, where St Patrick was born to a Romano-British family. Medieval writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> looked back to the Roman period, and the province became central to British archaeology and historical writing from the 18th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a CC BY 3.0 license., CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the late 3rd century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> faced growing raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> from the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scoti">Scotti</a> from Ireland. In 367 a combined attack, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> called a barbarian conspiracy, killed the Count of the Saxon Shore and captured the Duke, and order was restored only by Count Theodosius in 368–369. The province also suffered from its role in civil wars. In 383 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a> took troops to the continent to claim the throne, and after a series of short-lived usurpers in 406–407 the army proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iii-western-roman-emperor">Constantine III</a>, who crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with the field army. The coinage supply ceased in the early 5th century, and towns and villas, already declining, were largely abandoned within a generation. According to the Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>, around 409 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a> expelled Roman officials, and in 410 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> told the cities of Britain to look to their own defence, though some scholars think the letter was addressed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruttium">Bruttium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Roman rule was never restored. In the following decades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlers took over much of the east, while British successor kingdoms such as Powys and Dumnonia emerged in the west, keeping elements of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and culture.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> brought most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> for more than 350 years, creating the towns, roads and frontier walls that shaped the island's geography, producing emperors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, and leaving a legacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and urban sites that survived the collapse of Roman rule around 410.</em></p>

